'''Beatrice "Bice" Lazzari''' (15 November 1900 – 13 November 1981) was an [[Italian painter]].<ref name="Clara">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari |url=http://clara.nmwa.org/index.php?g=entity_detail&entity_id=4985 |website=Clara: Database of Women Artists. |access-date=5 July 2024}}</ref>

==Early life==
'''Beatrice Lazzari''' was born in [[Venice]] to Lorenzo (Luciano) Lazzari and Francesca Rinaldo. She was the second of three sisters, the youngest of whom, Onorina (Nini), married architect [[Carlo Scarpa]]. Beatrice was educated in Venice, first at the [[Benedetto Marcello Conservatoire|Benedetto Marcello Conservatory]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=KIZOAAAAYAAJ&q=bice+Lazzari+Benedetto+Marcello+Conservatory|title=Lionello Venturi e l'avanguardia italiana|first1=Carlo Federico|last1=Teodoro|first2=Lionello|last2=Venturi|first3=Giovanni|last3=Spadolini|first4=Pavullo nel Frignano (Italy) Galleria comunale d'arte|last4=moderna|date=July 1, 1991|publisher=Artioli|via=Google Books}}</ref> and then at the [[Academy of Fine Arts|Accademia di Belle Arti di Venezia]], where she would later be invited back as a lecturer.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://advancingwomenartists.org/artists/beatrice-lazzari|title=Beatrice Lazzari|website=Advancing Women Artists}}</ref><ref name="guggenheim collection">{{Cite web |url=http://www.guggenheim-venice.it/inglese/collections/artisti/biografia.php?id_art=94 |title=Guggenheim |access-date=2019-10-12 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170709003801/http://www.guggenheim-venice.it/inglese/collections/artisti/biografia.php?id_art=94 |archive-date=2017-07-09 |url-status=dead }}

==The Venice Years 1924–1934==
</ref> Lazzari's first exhibition was a group show at the Bevilacqua La Masa Foundation in 1924.<ref name="guggenheim collection"/> In 1928 she had her first solo exhibition at Galleria Botteghe D'Arte in Venice. In 1929 she had her second solo shows at the Galleria San Moise. On both occasions, she mostly exhibited figurative paintings. In the early 1930s, Lazzari hung out at the Artistic Circle of Palazzo dei Piombi e dal Caffè on the banks of the Zattere, where she met [[Carlo Scarpa]], Mario Deluigi and [[Virgilio Guidi]] and was inspired to steer her work towards a more rationalist approach.<ref>Irene de Guttry, Maria Pia Maino, Mario Quesada, ''Le arti minori d’autore in Italia dal 1900 al 1930'', Bari, Italy: Laterza 1985</ref> She made a clean break from figuration, and started working on abstract and geometric compositions.

==The Rome Years 1935-1981==
In 1935 Lazzari moved to Rome where, on top regularly exhibiting her paintings in gallery and museum shows, she started working on murals and decorative panels in collaboration with the architects Attilio and [[Ernesto Lapadula]]. In 1941 she married the Venetian architect Diego Rosa.<ref name="Sothebys">{{cite web |last1=Ugolini |first1=Paula |title=Bice Lazzari: 'Like a Piece of Ice in Which a Flame Burns' |url=https://www.sothebys.com/en/articles/bice-lazzari-like-a-piece-of-ice-in-which-a-flame-burns |website=Sothebys |access-date=5 July 2024 |language=en |date=12 January 2018}}</ref>

After [[World War Two]], Lazzari focused exclusively on painting. In the 1950s she was invited to participate in the [[25th Venice Biennale]] and the [[Rome Quadrennial]]. From the late 1950s to 1963, she worked mostly with oil to reinforce the application of other materials such as glues, sands and later acrylics.<ref name="AWARE">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari |url=https://awarewomenartists.com/en/artiste/bice-lazzari/ |website=AWARE Women artists / Femmes artistes |access-date=5 July 2024}}</ref> In 1964 her work took a minimalist turn, her work often consisting in graphite-drawned lines on monochrome backgrounds. Lazzari died in Rome in 1981.

==Legacy==
The Archive Bice Lazzari is based in Rome. It houses a significant number of her works, including paintings, poems and exhibition catalogues.<ref name="Associazione Archivio Bice Lazzari">{{cite web |last1= |title=Archivio Bice Lazzari |url=https://www.archiviobicelazzari.com/en/ |website=Associazione Archivio Bice Lazzari |access-date=5 July 2024}}</ref>

From May 10 to Sep 22, 2013, the [[National Museum of Women in the Arts]] exhibited ''Bice Lazzari: Signature Line'', displaying paintings and drawings from the Archivio Bice Lazzari.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bice Lazzari: Signature Line {{!}} Exhibition |url=https://nmwa.org/exhibitions/bice-lazzari-signature-line/ |access-date=2026-03-24 |website=National Museum of Women in the Arts |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 2018-2019 [[The Phillips Collection]] exhibited ''Bice Lazzari: The Poetry of Mark-Making'', featuring works acquired by the Collection.  The exhibition was curated by [[Renato Miracco]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2018-10-23 |title=Bice Lazzari: The Poetry of Mark-Making {{!}} The Phillips Collection |url=https://www.phillipscollection.org/event/2018-10-22-bice-lazzari-poetry-mark-making |access-date=2025-08-11 |website=www.phillipscollection.org |language=en}}</ref>

Lazzari's work was included in the 2021 exhibition ''[[Women in Abstraction]]'' at the [[Centre Pompidou]].<ref name="Women in abstraction">{{cite book |title=Women in abstraction |date=2021 |publisher=Thames & Hudson Ltd.; Thames & Hudson Inc |location=London : New York, New York |isbn=978-0500094372 |pages=170}}</ref> In 2022 the [[Estorick Collection of Modern Italian Art]] in London held a retrospective of her work entitled ''Bice Lazzari: Modernist Pioneer''.<ref name="Estorick Collection">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari: Modernist Pioneer |url=https://www.estorickcollection.com/exhibitions/bice-lazzari-modernist-pioneer |website=Estorick Collection |access-date=20 April 2023}}</ref><ref name="The Observer">{{cite web |last1=Cooke |first1=Rachel |title=Bice Lazzari: Modernist Pioneer review – exquisite, tranquil abstraction that draws you in |url=https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2022/jan/16/bice-lazzari-modernist-pioneer-estorick-collection-london-review-postwar-italy |website=The Observer |access-date=20 April 2023 |date=16 January 2022}}</ref><ref name="Apollo Magazine">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari: Modernist Pioneer |url=https://www.apollo-magazine.com/bice-lazzari-modernist-pioneer/ |website=Apollo Magazine |access-date=20 April 2023 |date=7 January 2022}}</ref>

In 2023 her work was included in the exhibition ''[[Action, Gesture, Paint: Women Artists and Global Abstraction 1940-1970]]'' at the [[Whitechapel Gallery]] in London.<ref name="Whitechapel Gallery">{{cite web |title=Action, Gesture, Paint |url=https://www.whitechapelgallery.org/exhibitions/action-gesture-paint-women-and-global-abstraction-1940-70/ |website=Whitechapel Gallery |access-date=20 April 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

Lazzari's work is in the National Museum of Women in the Arts<ref name="NMWA">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari: Signature Line |url=https://nmwa.org/exhibitions/bice-lazzari-signature-line/ |website=NMWA |access-date=5 July 2024}}</ref> and [[The Phillips Collection]] in Washington, D.C.<ref name="Phillips Collection">{{cite web |title=Bice Lazzari |url=https://www.phillipscollection.org/bice-lazzari |website=Phillips Collection |access-date=20 April 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

